## 5. CORS Policy Not Defined

### Issue

The app currently does not use CORS middleware. That means browsers will apply default same-origin restrictions.

This is not necessarily vulnerable by itself. However, if CORS is added later using a permissive config such as:

```js
app.use(cors());
```

or:

```js
app.use(cors({ origin: '*' }));
```

then sensitive endpoints may become accessible from arbitrary web origins.

### Recommendation

If browser clients need to access the API, define an explicit allowlist.

Example:

```js
const cors = require('cors');

const allowedOrigins = [
  'https://example.com',
  'https://admin.example.com',
];

app.use(cors({
  origin(origin, callback) {
    if (!origin || allowedOrigins.includes(origin)) {
      return callback(null, true);
    }

    return callback(new Error('Not allowed by CORS'));
  },
  methods: ['GET'],
  allowedHeaders: ['Authorization', 'Content-Type'],
}));
```

If this API is not meant for browsers, avoid enabling CORS.

