## 7. No CORS policy

### Severity: Low to Medium

The app does not configure CORS. By default, browsers will block cross-origin JavaScript access unless CORS headers are added. This is safer than allowing all origins.

However, if CORS is later enabled broadly, misconfiguration could expose sensitive endpoints.

### OWASP category

- A05: Security Misconfiguration

### Recommendation

If CORS is needed, explicitly allow trusted origins.

Install:

```bash
npm install cors
```

Use:

```js
const cors = require('cors');

app.use(cors({
  origin: ['https://example.com', 'https://admin.example.com'],
  methods: ['GET'],
  allowedHeaders: ['Content-Type', 'Authorization'],
}));
```

Avoid this in production if protected endpoints exist:

```js
app.use(cors({ origin: '*' }));
```

---

## 8. No centralized error handler

### Severity: Low currently

The current route handlers are simple and unlikely to throw, but as the app grows, uncaught errors may expose implementation details or produce inconsistent responses.

### OWASP category

- A05: Security Misconfiguration

### Recommendation

Add a consistent error handler.

```js
app.use((err, req, res, next) => {
  console.error(err);

  res.status(err.status || 500).json({
    error: 'Internal Server Error',
  });
});
```

For 404 handling:

```js
app.use((req, res) => {
  res.status(404).json({ error: 'Not Found' });
});
```

Avoid sending stack traces to clients in production.

---

## 9. Express fingerprinting

### Severity: Low

Express can expose the `X-Powered-By` header unless disabled.

### OWASP category

- A05: Security Misconfiguration

### Recommendation

Disable it:

```js
app.disable('x-powered-by');
```

Helmet may also help with related hardening.

---

## 10. Server starts automatically on import

### Severity: Low

This line starts the server whenever the module is imported:

```js
app.listen(3000, () => {
  console.log('Server running on port 3000');
});
```

This can make testing harder and may cause accidental server startup in tooling contexts.

### Recommendation

Only listen when the file is run directly.

```js
if (require.main === module) {
  const port = process.env.PORT || 3000;

  app.listen(port, () => {
    console.log(`Server running on port ${port}`);
  });
}

module.exports = app;
```

This is not a major vulnerability, but it is a safer application structure.

---

## 11. Hardcoded port

### Severity: Low

The app listens on port `3000` unconditionally.

```js
app.listen(3000, () => {
  console.log('Server running on port 3000');
});
```

### Recommendation

Use environment-based configuration.

```js
const port = Number(process.env.PORT) || 3000;

app.listen(port, () => {
  console.log(`Server running on port ${port}`);
});
```

