# 8. Health endpoint may disclose internal timing details

## Issue

The `/health` endpoint returns the current server timestamp:

```js
res.json({ status: 'ok', timestamp: new Date().toISOString() });
```

This is not highly sensitive, but it can reveal server time. In some systems, timing information can help attackers with replay attacks, token timing, clock skew assumptions, or fingerprinting.

## Recommendation

For a public health check, keep it minimal:

```js
app.get('/health', (req, res) => {
  res.json({ status: 'ok' });
});
```

For deeper health checks, expose them only internally or behind authentication:

```js
app.get('/internal/health', requireInternalAuth, async (req, res) => {
  res.json({
    status: 'ok',
    database: 'ok',
    cache: 'ok',
  });
});
```

---

# 9. No centralized error handling

## Issue

The app currently has no error handler. As the application grows, unhandled errors may leak stack traces in development or produce inconsistent responses.

## Recommendation

Add a generic error handler at the end of the middleware chain:

```js
app.use((err, req, res, next) => {
  console.error(err);

  res.status(500).json({
    error: 'Internal server error',
  });
});
```

Avoid returning raw error messages to clients.

---

# 10. No 404 handler

## Issue

Unknown routes will receive Express’s default 404 response. This is not usually dangerous, but consistent JSON API responses are better.

## Recommendation

Add:

```js
app.use((req, res) => {
  res.status(404).json({ error: 'Not found' });
});
```

Place this after all real routes and before the error handler.

---

# 11. Hardcoded port

## Issue

The app listens on a hardcoded port:

```js
app.listen(3000, () => {
  console.log('Server running on port 3000');
});
```

This is not a direct vulnerability, but it reduces deployment flexibility and can cause operational issues.

## Recommendation

Use an environment variable:

```js
const port = Number(process.env.PORT) || 3000;

app.listen(port, () => {
  console.log(`Server running on port ${port}`);
});
```

---

# 12. Server starts when module is imported

## Issue

The app calls `listen()` directly in `app.js`. This makes the server start whenever the module is imported, including during tests.

```js
module.exports = app;
```

This is more of a maintainability and test isolation issue than a direct vulnerability, but poor testability can reduce security test coverage.

## Recommendation

Separate app creation from server startup.

Example `app.js`:

```js
const express = require('express');

const app = express();

app.get('/health', (req, res) => {
  res.json({ status: 'ok' });
});

module.exports = app;
```

Example `server.js`:

```js
const app = require('./app');

const port = Number(process.env.PORT) || 3000;

app.listen(port, () => {
  console.log(`Server running on port ${port}`);
});
```

---

# 13. HTTPS and reverse proxy considerations

## Issue

The app itself does not enforce HTTPS. In many deployments, TLS is terminated by a load balancer or reverse proxy, which is fine, but the app should be configured correctly behind that proxy.

## Recommendation

If deployed behind a trusted proxy:

```js
app.set('trust proxy', 1);
```

Use HSTS via Helmet:

```js
app.use(helmet.hsts({
  maxAge: 15552000,
  includeSubDomains: true,
}));
```

If you want to redirect HTTP to HTTPS behind a proxy:

```js
function requireHttps(req, res, next) {
  if (req.secure || req.get('x-forwarded-proto') === 'https') {
    return next();
  }

  return res.redirect(301, `https://${req.get('host')}${req.originalUrl}`);
}
```

Only use this when proxy headers are trusted and properly configured.

---

# 14. CORS

## Issue

The app does not configure CORS. By default, browsers will not allow arbitrary cross-origin JavaScript to read responses unless CORS headers are set.

This is usually safer than enabling permissive CORS.

## Recommendation

Do not add broad CORS like this in production:

```js
app.use(cors());
```

Instead, if needed, allow specific origins:

```js
const cors = require('cors');

app.use(cors({
  origin: ['https://example.com', 'https://admin.example.com'],
  methods: ['GET'],
  allowedHeaders: ['Authorization', 'Content-Type'],
}));
```

---

# 15. Logging and auditability

## Issue

Access to `/users` is not logged. For sensitive endpoints, you generally want logs for monitoring and incident response.

## Recommendation

Use structured logging and avoid logging secrets.

Example:

```bash
npm install pino-http
```

```js
const pinoHttp = require('pino-http');

app.use(pinoHttp({
  redact: ['req.headers.authorization', 'req.headers.cookie', 'req.headers["x-api-key"]'],
}));
```

Then log sensitive access events:

```js
app.get('/users', requireAuth, requireRole('admin'), (req, res) => {
  req.log.info({ userId: req.user.sub }, 'users list accessed');
  res.json(users);
});
```

---

# Suggested hardened version

Example improved `app.js`:

```js
const express = require('express');
const helmet = require('helmet');
const rateLimit = require('express-rate-limit');

const app = express();

app.disable('x-powered-by');

app.use(helmet({
  contentSecurityPolicy: false,
}));

app.use(express.json({ limit: '100kb' }));

const generalLimiter = rateLimit({
  windowMs: 15 * 60 * 1000,
  max: 300,
  standardHeaders: true,
  legacyHeaders: false,
});

const sensitiveLimiter = rateLimit({
  windowMs: 15 * 60 * 1000,
  max: 50,
  standardHeaders: true,
  legacyHeaders: false,
});

app.use(generalLimiter);

const users = [
  { id: 1, name: 'Alice', email: 'alice@example.com' },
  { id: 2, name: 'Bob', email: 'bob@example.com' },
];

function requireApiKey(req, res, next) {
  const expectedKey = process.env.API_KEY;
  const providedKey = req.get('x-api-key');

  if (!expectedKey) {
    return res.status(500).json({ error: 'Server authentication not configured' });
  }

  if (!providedKey || providedKey !== expectedKey) {
    return res.status(401).json({ error: 'Unauthorized' });
  }

  next();
}

app.get('/health', (req, res) => {
  res.json({ status: 'ok' });
});

app.get('/users', sensitiveLimiter, requireApiKey, (req, res) => {
  // Only return sensitive fields if the caller is authorized to see them.
  res.json(users.map(user => ({
    id: user.id,
    name: user.name,
    email: user.email,
  })));
});

app.use((req, res) => {
  res.status(404).json({ error: 'Not found' });
});

app.use((err, req, res, next) => {
  console.error(err);

  res.status(500).json({
    error: 'Internal server error',
  });
});

module.exports = app;
```

And a separate `server.js`:

```js
const app = require('./app');

const port = Number(process.env.PORT) || 3000;

app.listen(port, () => {
  console.log(`Server running on port ${port}`);
});
```

Install required packages:

```bash
npm install helmet express-rate-limit
```

