# OWASP Top 10 Review

## 1. Broken Access Control

### Issue

The `/users` endpoint returns user records without authentication or authorization:

```js
app.get('/users', (req, res) => {
  res.json([
    { id: 1, name: 'Alice', email: 'alice@example.com' },
    { id: 2, name: 'Bob', email: 'bob@example.com' },
  ]);
});
```

Anyone who can reach the service can access all users.

### Risk

This maps directly to **OWASP A01: Broken Access Control**.

Even though the data is hardcoded here, in a real application this would represent an unauthenticated data disclosure.

### Recommendation

Require authentication before serving user data.

Example using JWT middleware:

```js
const jwt = require('jsonwebtoken');

function requireAuth(req, res, next) {
  const authHeader = req.headers.authorization;

  if (!authHeader || !authHeader.startsWith('Bearer ')) {
    return res.status(401).json({ error: 'Authentication required' });
  }

  const token = authHeader.slice('Bearer '.length);

  try {
    const payload = jwt.verify(token, process.env.JWT_SECRET);
    req.user = payload;
    return next();
  } catch {
    return res.status(401).json({ error: 'Invalid or expired token' });
  }
}
```

Then protect `/users`:

```js
app.get('/users', requireAuth, (req, res) => {
  res.json([
    { id: 1, name: 'Alice' },
    { id: 2, name: 'Bob' },
  ]);
});
```

If email addresses are not needed, do not return them.

---

## 2. Cryptographic Failures / Sensitive Data Exposure

### Issue

The `/users` route exposes email addresses:

```js
{ id: 1, name: 'Alice', email: 'alice@example.com' }
```

Emails are personally identifiable information.

### Risk

This maps to **OWASP A02: Cryptographic Failures**, specifically exposure of sensitive information.

### Recommendations

Return only the fields required by the client.

Prefer:

```js
app.get('/users', requireAuth, (req, res) => {
  res.json([
    { id: 1, name: 'Alice' },
    { id: 2, name: 'Bob' },
  ]);
});
```

If the requester is allowed to see emails, enforce authorization:

```js
function requireRole(role) {
  return (req, res, next) => {
    if (!req.user || req.user.role !== role) {
      return res.status(403).json({ error: 'Forbidden' });
    }

    next();
  };
}

app.get('/users', requireAuth, requireRole('admin'), (req, res) => {
  res.json([
    { id: 1, name: 'Alice', email: 'alice@example.com' },
    { id: 2, name: 'Bob', email: 'bob@example.com' },
  ]);
});
```

